For an object floating in a fluid, the weight of the object is equal to the buoyant force acting on it.
Mg = F_{B}
Where \(M\) is the mass of the cube, \(g\) is the acceleration due to gravity, and \(F_{B}\) is the buoyant force, which is equal to the weight of the displaced fluid (\(\rho_{water} \times V_{d} \times g\)), where \(V_{d}\) is the volume of the cube submerged in water.
Mg = \rho_{water} V_{d} g
M = \rho_{water} V_{d}
Substituting the given values (converting mass to kg):
(400 \times 10^{-3})\ \text{kg} = (10^{3}\ \text{kg m}^{-3}) \times V_{d}
V_{d} = \frac{400 \times 10^{-3}}{10^{3}} = 400 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{m}^{3}
The total volume of the cube is:
V_{total} = (\text{edge})^{3} = (10\ \text{cm})^{3} = 1000\ \text{cm}^{3} = 1000 \times (10^{-2}\ \text{m})^{3} = 10^{-3}\ \text{m}^{3}
The volume of the cube outside the water is the total volume minus the submerged volume:
V_{outside} = V_{total} - V_{d}
V_{outside} = 10^{-3} - 400 \times 10^{-6} = 1000 \times 10^{-6} - 400 \times 10^{-6}
V_{outside} = 600 \times 10^{-6}\ \text{m}^{3}
Converting this volume to \(\text{cm}^{3}\):
V_{outside} = 600 \times 10^{-6} \times (10^{2}\ \text{cm})^{3} = 600 \times 10^{-6} \times 10^{6}\ \text{cm}^{3} = 600\ \text{cm}^{3}

Given, the ratio of vapour densities of two gases at the same temperature is:
\frac{\rho_1}{\rho_2} = \frac{4}{25}
According to Graham's Law of Diffusion/Effusion, the ratio of r.m.s. velocities is inversely proportional to the square root of their vapour densities (or molecular masses) at the same temperature.
\frac{v_{\text{rms},1}}{v_{\text{rms},2}} = \sqrt{\frac{\rho_2}{\rho_1}}
Substituting the given ratio:
\frac{v_{\text{rms},1}}{v_{\text{rms},2}} = \sqrt{\frac{25}{4}} = \frac{5}{2}
Thus, the ratio of r.m.s. velocities will be \(\frac{5}{2}\).

