### Given Data

Refractive index of liquid A, \(n_1 = 1.3\)
Refractive index of liquid B, \(n_2 = 1.4\)
Object distance, \(u = -13\text{ cm}\) (object is in medium A, and placed to the left of the vertex)
Magnification, \(m = -2\)

### Diagram of the setup


### Applying the refraction formula at a spherical surface

\frac{n_2}{v} - \frac{n_1}{u} = \frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}
Substitute the given values:
\frac{1.4}{v} - \frac{1.3}{-13} = \frac{1.4 - 1.3}{R}
\frac{1.4}{v} + \frac{1}{10} = \frac{0.1}{R}
\frac{1.4}{v} = \frac{0.1}{R} - \frac{1}{10} = \frac{1}{10R} - \frac{1}{10} = \frac{1-R}{10R} \quad \ldots(1)

### Using the magnification formula

The magnification for refraction at a spherical surface is given by:
m = \frac{v/n_2}{u/n_1}
Substitute the given magnification and refractive indices:
-2 = \frac{v/1.4}{-13/1.3}
-2 = \frac{v/1.4}{-10}
20 = \frac{v}{1.4}
v = 20 \times 1.4 = 28\text{ cm}

### Calculating the radius of curvature (R)

Substitute the value of \(v\) into equation (1):
\frac{1.4}{28} = \frac{1-R}{10R}
\frac{1}{20} = \frac{1-R}{10R}
10R = 20(1-R)
10R = 20 - 20R
30R = 20
R = \frac{20}{30} = \frac{2}{3}\text{ cm}
The radius of curvature of the meniscus is \(\frac{2}{3}\text{ cm}\).

